1
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:12,910
In 1915,

2
00:00:12,910 --> 00:00:15,190
deep in the frozen seas of Antarctica,

3
00:00:15,190 --> 00:00:17,830
a wooden ship was slowly crushed by the ice,

4
00:00:17,830 --> 00:00:20,155
the way you would crush an eggshell

5
00:00:20,305 --> 00:00:22,615
Twenty-eight men were left stranded on drifting

6
00:00:22,615 --> 00:00:22,975
ice,

7
00:00:22,975 --> 00:00:24,575
in the coldest place on the planet

8
00:00:24,575 --> 00:00:27,019
No one in the world knew where they were

9
00:00:27,169 --> 00:00:27,599
And yet,

10
00:00:27,599 --> 00:00:29,439
two years after leaving England,

11
00:00:29,439 --> 00:00:32,039
all twenty-eight of them came home alive

12
00:00:32,713 --> 00:00:34,303
This is the story of Endurance,

13
00:00:34,303 --> 00:00:36,383
told with stick figures

14
00:00:41,767 --> 00:00:42,797
In 1911,

15
00:00:42,797 --> 00:00:45,064
Norway's Roald Amundsen reached the South Pole

16
00:00:45,064 --> 00:00:46,677
The race to the end of the Earth was

17
00:00:46,677 --> 00:00:47,797
over

18
00:00:48,634 --> 00:00:50,784
But the explorer Ernest Shackleton wanted

19
00:00:50,784 --> 00:00:52,024
something even bolder:

20
00:00:52,024 --> 00:00:54,624
to walk across the entire Antarctic continent

21
00:00:56,431 --> 00:00:58,541
Nearly three thousand kilometres of ice and

22
00:00:58,541 --> 00:00:58,901
snow,

23
00:00:58,901 --> 00:00:59,861
from the Weddell Sea,

24
00:00:59,861 --> 00:01:01,101
across the South Pole,

25
00:01:01,101 --> 00:01:03,701
to the Ross Sea on the other side

26
00:01:04,717 --> 00:01:06,947
He bought a three-masted wooden ship built in

27
00:01:06,947 --> 00:01:07,494
Norway,

28
00:01:07,494 --> 00:01:08,907
forty-four metres long,

29
00:01:08,907 --> 00:01:11,507
one of the strongest of its day

30
00:01:12,060 --> 00:01:13,890
He renamed her after his family motto:

31
00:01:13,890 --> 00:01:15,170
by endurance we conquer

32
00:01:15,170 --> 00:01:17,250
The ship became Endurance

33
00:01:18,408 --> 00:01:20,558
More than five thousand people applied to go

34
00:01:20,558 --> 00:01:22,758
Shackleton picked his men in his own way:

35
00:01:22,758 --> 00:01:25,078
he once asked a physicist whether he could

36
00:01:25,078 --> 00:01:26,198
sing

37
00:01:27,194 --> 00:01:28,464
As for the famous advert,

38
00:01:28,464 --> 00:01:30,264
men wanted for a hazardous journey,

39
00:01:30,264 --> 00:01:31,624
safe return doubtful,

40
00:01:31,624 --> 00:01:33,304
no one has ever found the original

41
00:01:33,304 --> 00:01:35,904
It is most likely a legend

42
00:01:36,699 --> 00:01:38,249
In August 1914,

43
00:01:38,249 --> 00:01:40,129
just as the ship was about to sail,

44
00:01:40,129 --> 00:01:41,609
the First World War broke out

45
00:01:41,609 --> 00:01:43,929
Shackleton offered the ship and its crew to the

46
00:01:43,929 --> 00:01:45,049
Navy

47
00:01:46,088 --> 00:01:48,518
The Admiralty replied with a single word:

48
00:01:48,518 --> 00:01:48,998
proceed

49
00:01:48,998 --> 00:01:50,438
On the eighth of August,

50
00:01:50,438 --> 00:01:51,798
1914,

51
00:01:51,798 --> 00:01:53,558
Endurance left Plymouth

52
00:01:59,767 --> 00:02:00,837
In Buenos Aires,

53
00:02:00,837 --> 00:02:03,197
a young sailor named Perce Blackborow was

54
00:02:03,197 --> 00:02:03,777
turned down

55
00:02:03,777 --> 00:02:05,237
He stowed away on board,

56
00:02:05,237 --> 00:02:07,837
and was only found three days later

57
00:02:08,736 --> 00:02:09,886
Shackleton was furious,

58
00:02:09,886 --> 00:02:10,806
and warned him:

59
00:02:10,806 --> 00:02:13,166
if the expedition ran out of food,

60
00:02:13,166 --> 00:02:15,046
stowaways would be the first to be eaten

61
00:02:15,046 --> 00:02:15,946
In the end,

62
00:02:15,946 --> 00:02:18,026
Blackborow joined the crew

63
00:02:18,866 --> 00:02:20,536
In November 1914,

64
00:02:20,536 --> 00:02:22,496
the ship called at Grytviken,

65
00:02:22,496 --> 00:02:24,656
a whaling station on South Georgia,

66
00:02:24,656 --> 00:02:27,256
the last inhabited place on the route

67
00:02:28,450 --> 00:02:30,253
The whalers warned that the ice in the Weddell

68
00:02:30,253 --> 00:02:32,853
Sea was the worst they had ever seen

69
00:02:33,927 --> 00:02:35,117
On the fifth of December,

70
00:02:35,117 --> 00:02:36,397
1914,

71
00:02:36,397 --> 00:02:37,877
Endurance sailed anyway

72
00:02:37,877 --> 00:02:39,597
On board were twenty-eight men,

73
00:02:39,597 --> 00:02:40,837
sixty-nine sled dogs,

74
00:02:40,837 --> 00:02:43,437
and a cat called Mrs Chippy

75
00:02:44,280 --> 00:02:45,270
Just two days later,

76
00:02:45,270 --> 00:02:46,830
the ship met the pack ice

77
00:02:46,830 --> 00:02:47,950
For six weeks,

78
00:02:47,950 --> 00:02:49,830
Endurance wove between the floes,

79
00:02:49,830 --> 00:02:52,310
hunting for every lead of open water to push

80
00:02:52,310 --> 00:02:53,430
south

81
00:02:54,235 --> 00:02:55,745
On the eighteenth of January,

82
00:02:55,745 --> 00:02:56,785
1915,

83
00:02:56,785 --> 00:02:59,265
a north-east gale packed the ice tight around

84
00:02:59,265 --> 00:02:59,745
the ship

85
00:02:59,745 --> 00:03:00,665
In a single night,

86
00:03:00,665 --> 00:03:02,825
the sea around her turned into a field of

87
00:03:02,825 --> 00:03:03,945
ice

88
00:03:04,871 --> 00:03:06,381
One of the crew wrote that the ship was

89
00:03:06,381 --> 00:03:07,101
stuck fast,

90
00:03:07,101 --> 00:03:09,701
like an almond in a bar of chocolate

91
00:03:10,743 --> 00:03:12,973
Their destination was barely a day's sail away

92
00:03:12,973 --> 00:03:15,573
But Endurance would never be free again

93
00:03:21,833 --> 00:03:23,463
In February 1915,

94
00:03:23,463 --> 00:03:25,503
the crew attacked the ice with saws,

95
00:03:25,503 --> 00:03:26,503
chisels and picks,

96
00:03:26,503 --> 00:03:28,063
trying to cut a channel for the ship

97
00:03:28,063 --> 00:03:29,823
It was useless

98
00:03:30,628 --> 00:03:32,338
Shackleton announced that Endurance would

99
00:03:32,338 --> 00:03:33,618
become a winter station

100
00:03:33,618 --> 00:03:35,258
They would wait for the ice to break up

101
00:03:35,258 --> 00:03:36,698
in spring

102
00:03:37,367 --> 00:03:39,037
The dogs were moved onto the ice,

103
00:03:39,037 --> 00:03:41,277
into kennels made of snow around the ship,

104
00:03:41,277 --> 00:03:43,357
like a little village

105
00:03:44,021 --> 00:03:44,691
In early May,

106
00:03:44,691 --> 00:03:46,611
the sun set and did not rise again

107
00:03:46,611 --> 00:03:49,211
The polar night lasted for weeks on end

108
00:03:50,536 --> 00:03:51,846
To keep their spirits up,

109
00:03:51,846 --> 00:03:53,466
they played football on the ice,

110
00:03:53,466 --> 00:03:54,646
held dog races,

111
00:03:54,646 --> 00:03:57,246
and even shaved each other's heads for fun

112
00:03:58,648 --> 00:03:59,358
In the darkness,

113
00:03:59,358 --> 00:04:02,238
photographer Frank Hurley fired magnesium flash

114
00:04:02,238 --> 00:04:03,958
powder to photograph the frozen ship

115
00:04:03,958 --> 00:04:06,278
It became one of the most famous pictures ever

116
00:04:06,278 --> 00:04:08,038
taken in Antarctica

117
00:04:09,373 --> 00:04:11,023
Meanwhile the ice kept drifting,

118
00:04:11,023 --> 00:04:12,243
carrying the ship with it

119
00:04:12,243 --> 00:04:12,943
By October,

120
00:04:12,943 --> 00:04:14,997
Endurance had drifted nearly a thousand

121
00:04:14,997 --> 00:04:16,117
kilometres

122
00:04:17,211 --> 00:04:17,801
One night,

123
00:04:17,801 --> 00:04:20,521
Shackleton quietly told Captain Frank Worsley:

124
00:04:20,521 --> 00:04:21,841
the ship can't live in this

125
00:04:21,841 --> 00:04:22,881
What the ice gets,

126
00:04:22,881 --> 00:04:24,641
the ice keeps

127
00:04:30,733 --> 00:04:31,483
Spring arrived

128
00:04:31,483 --> 00:04:32,803
But instead of melting,

129
00:04:32,803 --> 00:04:35,403
the ice began to move and press in

130
00:04:36,567 --> 00:04:38,637
Floes weighing millions of tonnes ground

131
00:04:38,637 --> 00:04:39,277
against the hull

132
00:04:39,277 --> 00:04:40,357
The timbers groaned,

133
00:04:40,357 --> 00:04:42,957
and the beams snapped with loud cracks

134
00:04:44,201 --> 00:04:45,951
On the twenty-seventh of October,

135
00:04:45,951 --> 00:04:47,071
1915,

136
00:04:47,071 --> 00:04:48,591
water flooded into the hold

137
00:04:48,591 --> 00:04:51,191
Shackleton gave the order to abandon ship

138
00:04:52,661 --> 00:04:54,051
They pitched camp on the ice,

139
00:04:54,051 --> 00:04:55,491
right beside their dying ship

140
00:04:55,491 --> 00:04:56,571
Shackleton said:

141
00:04:56,571 --> 00:04:58,051
ship and stores have gone,

142
00:04:58,051 --> 00:05:00,451
so now we'll go home

143
00:05:01,110 --> 00:05:04,020
Each man could keep only about one kilogram of

144
00:05:04,020 --> 00:05:05,140
personal belongings

145
00:05:05,140 --> 00:05:07,460
Shackleton himself threw his gold coins into

146
00:05:07,460 --> 00:05:08,900
the snow

147
00:05:09,633 --> 00:05:11,663
But he kept meteorologist Leonard Hussey's

148
00:05:11,663 --> 00:05:12,183
banjo,

149
00:05:12,183 --> 00:05:14,583
calling it vital mental medicine

150
00:05:15,656 --> 00:05:17,046
Hurley waded into the flooded,

151
00:05:17,046 --> 00:05:19,086
freezing hold to rescue his glass plate

152
00:05:19,086 --> 00:05:19,606
negatives

153
00:05:19,606 --> 00:05:20,966
He kept just over a hundred,

154
00:05:20,966 --> 00:05:23,006
and smashed hundreds of others himself,

155
00:05:23,006 --> 00:05:24,886
so he would not be tempted to go back

156
00:05:24,886 --> 00:05:26,326
for them

157
00:05:27,054 --> 00:05:28,604
On the twenty-first of November,

158
00:05:28,604 --> 00:05:29,724
1915,

159
00:05:29,724 --> 00:05:30,884
the stern rose high,

160
00:05:30,884 --> 00:05:33,004
and Endurance slipped beneath the ice

161
00:05:33,004 --> 00:05:34,404
Shackleton simply said:

162
00:05:34,404 --> 00:05:35,124
she's going,

163
00:05:35,124 --> 00:05:36,244
boys

164
00:05:38,007 --> 00:05:39,917
Now they were alone on drifting ice,

165
00:05:39,917 --> 00:05:41,877
more than five hundred kilometres from the

166
00:05:41,877 --> 00:05:43,317
nearest land

167
00:05:49,767 --> 00:05:51,597
Their first camp was called Ocean Camp

168
00:05:51,597 --> 00:05:53,237
The tents were so thin that you could see

169
00:05:53,237 --> 00:05:55,317
the moon through them

170
00:05:55,833 --> 00:05:58,063
They hunted seals and penguins for food

171
00:05:58,063 --> 00:06:00,743
Seal blubber was burned as fuel in a homemade

172
00:06:00,743 --> 00:06:01,863
stove

173
00:06:02,759 --> 00:06:04,869
Three lifeboats had been dragged from the ship:

174
00:06:04,869 --> 00:06:05,909
the James Caird,

175
00:06:05,909 --> 00:06:08,189
the Dudley Docker and the Stancomb Wills,

176
00:06:08,189 --> 00:06:10,589
named after the expedition's sponsors

177
00:06:12,160 --> 00:06:14,270
Shackleton decided to march towards land,

178
00:06:14,270 --> 00:06:15,230
hauling the boats,

179
00:06:15,230 --> 00:06:16,630
each weighing nearly a tonne,

180
00:06:16,630 --> 00:06:18,070
on sledges

181
00:06:19,062 --> 00:06:20,812
The soft snow came up to their knees,

182
00:06:20,812 --> 00:06:22,612
and the ice was broken and ridged

183
00:06:22,612 --> 00:06:25,212
Each day they covered less than two kilometres

184
00:06:26,452 --> 00:06:27,042
The carpenter,

185
00:06:27,042 --> 00:06:28,082
Harry McNish,

186
00:06:28,082 --> 00:06:29,322
exhausted and angry,

187
00:06:29,322 --> 00:06:30,882
refused to haul any further

188
00:06:30,882 --> 00:06:33,068
Shackleton had to put down that small rebellion

189
00:06:33,068 --> 00:06:35,468
right there on the ice

190
00:06:36,201 --> 00:06:37,231
After seven days,

191
00:06:37,231 --> 00:06:38,591
Shackleton called a halt

192
00:06:38,591 --> 00:06:39,311
At this rate,

193
00:06:39,311 --> 00:06:41,511
they would be worn out long before they reached

194
00:06:41,511 --> 00:06:42,631
land

195
00:06:43,505 --> 00:06:45,175
They set up a new camp and named it

196
00:06:45,175 --> 00:06:45,815
Patience Camp

197
00:06:45,815 --> 00:06:46,455
This time,

198
00:06:46,455 --> 00:06:48,575
they would wait for the ice itself to drift

199
00:06:48,575 --> 00:06:49,695
north

200
00:06:55,367 --> 00:06:57,317
More than three months of waiting on a drifting

201
00:06:57,317 --> 00:06:58,437
floe

202
00:06:59,427 --> 00:07:00,217
Food ran low

203
00:07:00,217 --> 00:07:01,857
Meals were mostly seal meat,

204
00:07:01,857 --> 00:07:02,417
penguin,

205
00:07:02,417 --> 00:07:03,857
and blubber

206
00:07:04,704 --> 00:07:06,654
They read the few books they had left over

207
00:07:06,654 --> 00:07:07,294
and over,

208
00:07:07,294 --> 00:07:08,174
played cards,

209
00:07:08,174 --> 00:07:10,054
and described to each other the feasts they

210
00:07:10,054 --> 00:07:12,134
would eat back home

211
00:07:12,851 --> 00:07:14,281
On the twenty-ninth of February,

212
00:07:14,281 --> 00:07:15,521
1916,

213
00:07:15,521 --> 00:07:16,521
for the leap day,

214
00:07:16,521 --> 00:07:18,841
the whole camp was given three full meals

215
00:07:18,841 --> 00:07:20,601
A real holiday

216
00:07:21,776 --> 00:07:22,206
One day,

217
00:07:22,206 --> 00:07:24,686
a leopard seal burst onto the ice and chased

218
00:07:24,686 --> 00:07:25,286
one of the men

219
00:07:25,286 --> 00:07:27,286
Frank Wild brought it down just in time,

220
00:07:27,286 --> 00:07:29,886
and the camp had extra meat

221
00:07:30,504 --> 00:07:32,294
The floe grew smaller and thinner,

222
00:07:32,294 --> 00:07:33,294
and began to crack

223
00:07:33,294 --> 00:07:35,894
Beneath it lay thousands of metres of ocean

224
00:07:37,274 --> 00:07:38,064
Then one day,

225
00:07:38,064 --> 00:07:40,464
they saw distant mountain peaks on the horizon

226
00:07:40,464 --> 00:07:41,224
Land at last,

227
00:07:41,224 --> 00:07:43,824
but there was no way to walk there

228
00:07:44,659 --> 00:07:45,849
On the ninth of April,

229
00:07:45,849 --> 00:07:47,249
1916,

230
00:07:47,249 --> 00:07:49,289
the floe split right through the camp

231
00:07:49,289 --> 00:07:50,789
Shackleton gave the order:

232
00:07:50,789 --> 00:07:52,549
launch the boats!

233
00:07:58,467 --> 00:08:00,737
Twenty-eight men crammed into three open wooden

234
00:08:00,737 --> 00:08:01,137
boats,

235
00:08:01,137 --> 00:08:03,737
in a sea full of drifting ice

236
00:08:04,568 --> 00:08:05,038
At night,

237
00:08:05,038 --> 00:08:07,198
they hauled the boats onto a floe to sleep

238
00:08:07,198 --> 00:08:08,358
On the very first night,

239
00:08:08,358 --> 00:08:10,958
the floe cracked right under one of the tents

240
00:08:12,283 --> 00:08:14,473
A sailor fell into the water in his sleeping

241
00:08:14,473 --> 00:08:14,793
bag

242
00:08:14,793 --> 00:08:16,993
Shackleton reached down and hauled him out,

243
00:08:16,993 --> 00:08:19,593
just seconds before the crack slammed shut

244
00:08:20,918 --> 00:08:21,508
After that,

245
00:08:21,508 --> 00:08:23,548
they no longer dared to camp on the ice

246
00:08:23,548 --> 00:08:25,108
Icy waves broke over them,

247
00:08:25,108 --> 00:08:26,468
fresh water ran short,

248
00:08:26,468 --> 00:08:28,548
and many suffered frostbite

249
00:08:29,620 --> 00:08:32,220
Killer whales circled the tiny boats

250
00:08:33,378 --> 00:08:35,608
Many of them barely slept for days and nights

251
00:08:35,608 --> 00:08:37,048
on end

252
00:08:37,891 --> 00:08:39,361
On the fifteenth of April,

253
00:08:39,361 --> 00:08:40,561
1916,

254
00:08:40,561 --> 00:08:42,441
the boats reached Elephant Island

255
00:08:42,441 --> 00:08:44,401
For the first time in four hundred and

256
00:08:44,401 --> 00:08:45,241
ninety-seven days,

257
00:08:45,241 --> 00:08:47,641
they stood on solid ground

258
00:08:48,465 --> 00:08:50,295
Some scooped up pebbles from the beach,

259
00:08:50,295 --> 00:08:51,615
some burst out laughing,

260
00:08:51,615 --> 00:08:54,215
some staggered about as if they were drunk

261
00:08:55,177 --> 00:08:55,847
Blackborow,

262
00:08:55,847 --> 00:08:57,047
the young stowaway,

263
00:08:57,047 --> 00:08:58,647
had badly frostbitten feet

264
00:08:58,647 --> 00:09:00,727
Shackleton wanted him to be the first to step

265
00:09:00,727 --> 00:09:01,847
ashore

266
00:09:07,867 --> 00:09:09,577
Elephant Island was a bare rock,

267
00:09:09,577 --> 00:09:11,577
battered by gales all year round

268
00:09:11,577 --> 00:09:13,377
Its name came from the elephant seals that

269
00:09:13,377 --> 00:09:15,457
lived on its shore

270
00:09:15,895 --> 00:09:17,405
No ship ever passed this way

271
00:09:17,405 --> 00:09:18,485
If they simply waited,

272
00:09:18,485 --> 00:09:21,085
no one would ever come to rescue them

273
00:09:21,729 --> 00:09:23,919
Shackleton made an almost insane decision:

274
00:09:23,919 --> 00:09:26,119
to sail a small boat to South Georgia,

275
00:09:26,119 --> 00:09:28,119
thirteen hundred kilometres away,

276
00:09:28,119 --> 00:09:30,199
about eight hundred miles

277
00:09:31,328 --> 00:09:33,278
They would have to cross the most violent sea

278
00:09:33,278 --> 00:09:33,958
in the world,

279
00:09:33,958 --> 00:09:36,518
where storms circle Antarctica with nothing to

280
00:09:36,518 --> 00:09:37,958
stop them

281
00:09:38,671 --> 00:09:40,741
The carpenter McNish raised the sides of the

282
00:09:40,741 --> 00:09:41,501
James Caird,

283
00:09:41,501 --> 00:09:43,821
and built a deck from packing-case wood,

284
00:09:43,821 --> 00:09:45,901
canvas and sledge runners

285
00:09:47,001 --> 00:09:48,671
The seams were caulked with lamp wick,

286
00:09:48,671 --> 00:09:51,071
oil paint and seal blood

287
00:09:51,914 --> 00:09:53,944
They loaded about seven hundred kilograms of

288
00:09:53,944 --> 00:09:55,944
stones into the bottom as ballast,

289
00:09:55,944 --> 00:09:58,544
to keep the boat from capsizing

290
00:09:59,404 --> 00:10:00,514
Six men set off:

291
00:10:00,514 --> 00:10:00,994
Shackleton,

292
00:10:00,994 --> 00:10:01,954
Worsley,

293
00:10:01,954 --> 00:10:02,434
Crean,

294
00:10:02,434 --> 00:10:03,394
McNish,

295
00:10:03,394 --> 00:10:05,154
McCarthy and Vincent

296
00:10:06,320 --> 00:10:07,630
Twenty-two stayed behind,

297
00:10:07,630 --> 00:10:09,230
under the command of Frank Wild

298
00:10:09,230 --> 00:10:10,870
On the twenty-fourth of April,

299
00:10:10,870 --> 00:10:12,190
1916,

300
00:10:12,190 --> 00:10:14,790
the James Caird left the shore

301
00:10:20,867 --> 00:10:23,137
The James Caird was less than seven metres

302
00:10:23,137 --> 00:10:23,417
long

303
00:10:23,417 --> 00:10:26,017
Six men squeezed into its low cramped space,

304
00:10:26,017 --> 00:10:28,417
soaked through day and night

305
00:10:29,023 --> 00:10:30,613
The waves were as high as hills

306
00:10:30,613 --> 00:10:31,773
Spray froze solid,

307
00:10:31,773 --> 00:10:34,373
coating the boat in thick ice

308
00:10:35,020 --> 00:10:36,410
The boat grew heavier and threatened to

309
00:10:36,410 --> 00:10:36,970
capsize

310
00:10:36,970 --> 00:10:39,010
They had to crawl out onto the slippery deck

311
00:10:39,010 --> 00:10:41,610
and hack the ice away with an axe

312
00:10:42,537 --> 00:10:43,867
To find out where they were,

313
00:10:43,867 --> 00:10:46,047
Worsley had to measure the angle of the sun

314
00:10:46,047 --> 00:10:46,807
with a sextant

315
00:10:46,807 --> 00:10:48,007
In seventeen days,

316
00:10:48,007 --> 00:10:50,407
he managed only four sightings

317
00:10:51,606 --> 00:10:53,076
Two men had to hold him tight,

318
00:10:53,076 --> 00:10:54,996
so he could keep his balance and sight the

319
00:10:54,996 --> 00:10:55,276
sun

320
00:10:55,276 --> 00:10:56,276
One small error,

321
00:10:56,276 --> 00:10:58,116
and they would drift out into the open

322
00:10:58,116 --> 00:10:59,236
Atlantic

323
00:11:00,373 --> 00:11:00,843
One night,

324
00:11:00,843 --> 00:11:03,323
Shackleton saw a white line in the sky and

325
00:11:03,323 --> 00:11:04,683
thought the clouds were clearing

326
00:11:04,683 --> 00:11:05,203
In fact,

327
00:11:05,203 --> 00:11:07,803
it was the crest of an enormous wave

328
00:11:08,675 --> 00:11:09,825
They bailed like madmen

329
00:11:09,825 --> 00:11:11,905
The boat stayed afloat

330
00:11:12,852 --> 00:11:13,842
On the eighth of May,

331
00:11:13,842 --> 00:11:15,162
1916,

332
00:11:15,162 --> 00:11:17,522
they sighted the black cliffs of South Georgia

333
00:11:17,522 --> 00:11:18,842
But a violent storm hit,

334
00:11:18,842 --> 00:11:21,442
and held them offshore for two more days

335
00:11:22,641 --> 00:11:23,631
On the tenth of May,

336
00:11:23,631 --> 00:11:25,031
1916,

337
00:11:25,031 --> 00:11:27,431
the James Caird landed in a small cove

338
00:11:27,431 --> 00:11:28,831
They knelt beside a stream,

339
00:11:28,831 --> 00:11:30,911
and drank fresh water

340
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:38,990
But the whaling stations were on the far side

341
00:11:38,990 --> 00:11:40,750
of the island

342
00:11:41,478 --> 00:11:42,508
The boat was too battered,

343
00:11:42,508 --> 00:11:44,228
and three of the men were too weak to

344
00:11:44,228 --> 00:11:46,308
go on by sea

345
00:11:46,674 --> 00:11:47,024
Shackleton,

346
00:11:47,024 --> 00:11:49,744
Worsley and Crean decided to cross the icy

347
00:11:49,744 --> 00:11:50,744
mountains on foot,

348
00:11:50,744 --> 00:11:53,344
mountains no one had ever crossed

349
00:11:54,110 --> 00:11:55,700
They had no map and no tent

350
00:11:55,700 --> 00:11:56,447
Only a rope,

351
00:11:56,447 --> 00:11:57,060
an adze,

352
00:11:57,060 --> 00:11:59,340
and screws driven into the soles of their boots

353
00:11:59,340 --> 00:12:01,420
to grip the ice

354
00:12:01,890 --> 00:12:03,560
As night fell on a mountainside,

355
00:12:03,560 --> 00:12:05,280
they coiled the rope into a seat,

356
00:12:05,280 --> 00:12:06,400
held on to one another,

357
00:12:06,400 --> 00:12:09,000
and slid straight down the slope

358
00:12:09,750 --> 00:12:10,300
Near dawn,

359
00:12:10,300 --> 00:12:11,760
the other two fell asleep

360
00:12:11,760 --> 00:12:13,900
Shackleton let them sleep for five minutes,

361
00:12:13,900 --> 00:12:16,020
then woke them and told them they had slept

362
00:12:16,020 --> 00:12:18,100
for half an hour

363
00:12:18,629 --> 00:12:19,579
At seven in the morning,

364
00:12:19,579 --> 00:12:21,179
they heard the steam whistle calling the

365
00:12:21,179 --> 00:12:22,979
workers at Stromness whaling station

366
00:12:22,979 --> 00:12:24,939
The first sound of the human world in nearly

367
00:12:24,939 --> 00:12:27,339
a year and a half

368
00:12:27,809 --> 00:12:29,919
After thirty-six hours without stopping,

369
00:12:29,919 --> 00:12:30,759
three ragged,

370
00:12:30,759 --> 00:12:32,399
bearded men walked into the station

371
00:12:32,399 --> 00:12:34,159
The manager knew Shackleton well,

372
00:12:34,159 --> 00:12:36,759
yet he did not recognise him

373
00:12:37,623 --> 00:12:37,983
Later,

374
00:12:37,983 --> 00:12:39,583
all three said that on that journey,

375
00:12:39,583 --> 00:12:41,583
they kept feeling that a fourth person was

376
00:12:41,583 --> 00:12:43,343
walking with them

377
00:12:49,067 --> 00:12:49,977
The very next day,

378
00:12:49,977 --> 00:12:52,457
a whaling ship sailed around the island to pick

379
00:12:52,457 --> 00:12:54,857
up the other three men

380
00:12:55,191 --> 00:12:57,261
But rescuing the twenty-two on Elephant Island

381
00:12:57,261 --> 00:12:58,021
was far harder

382
00:12:58,021 --> 00:12:58,981
Winter had arrived,

383
00:12:58,981 --> 00:13:01,581
and ice was closing in around the island

384
00:13:02,651 --> 00:13:03,481
The first attempt,

385
00:13:03,481 --> 00:13:04,561
on the Southern Sky,

386
00:13:04,561 --> 00:13:05,721
was stopped by ice

387
00:13:05,721 --> 00:13:06,361
The second,

388
00:13:06,361 --> 00:13:07,761
on a ship from Uruguay,

389
00:13:07,761 --> 00:13:08,601
had to turn back

390
00:13:08,601 --> 00:13:09,161
The third,

391
00:13:09,161 --> 00:13:10,361
on a wooden schooner,

392
00:13:10,361 --> 00:13:11,801
failed too

393
00:13:12,992 --> 00:13:13,942
On Elephant Island,

394
00:13:13,942 --> 00:13:16,582
the twenty-two men turned two boats upside down

395
00:13:16,582 --> 00:13:17,382
to make a roof,

396
00:13:17,382 --> 00:13:19,422
and lived crammed beneath them for more than

397
00:13:19,422 --> 00:13:20,862
four months

398
00:13:21,729 --> 00:13:24,292
The expedition's two doctors had to operate on

399
00:13:24,292 --> 00:13:25,599
Blackborow's frostbitten foot,

400
00:13:25,599 --> 00:13:27,119
right there in the dark hut,

401
00:13:27,119 --> 00:13:29,719
with the little anaesthetic they had left

402
00:13:30,793 --> 00:13:31,383
Every morning,

403
00:13:31,383 --> 00:13:33,703
Frank Wild rolled up his sleeping bag and

404
00:13:33,703 --> 00:13:34,343
called out:

405
00:13:34,343 --> 00:13:35,383
lash up and stow,

406
00:13:35,383 --> 00:13:35,823
boys,

407
00:13:35,823 --> 00:13:38,223
the Boss may come today!

408
00:13:39,033 --> 00:13:40,423
On the thirtieth of August,

409
00:13:40,423 --> 00:13:41,663
1916,

410
00:13:41,663 --> 00:13:42,823
on the fourth attempt,

411
00:13:42,823 --> 00:13:43,583
the Yelcho,

412
00:13:43,583 --> 00:13:46,330
a Chilean Navy tug commanded by Captain Luis

413
00:13:46,330 --> 00:13:46,743
Pardo,

414
00:13:46,743 --> 00:13:49,143
made it through the ice

415
00:13:49,681 --> 00:13:50,351
From the boat,

416
00:13:50,351 --> 00:13:52,631
Shackleton counted every man on the shore and

417
00:13:52,631 --> 00:13:53,111
shouted:

418
00:13:53,111 --> 00:13:53,991
are you all well?

419
00:13:53,991 --> 00:13:55,231
The answer rang back:

420
00:13:55,231 --> 00:13:56,031
all safe,

421
00:13:56,031 --> 00:13:57,471
all well!

422
00:13:58,408 --> 00:13:59,398
Within about an hour,

423
00:13:59,398 --> 00:14:00,438
everyone was aboard

424
00:14:00,438 --> 00:14:03,038
Not a single man was left behind

425
00:14:08,967 --> 00:14:11,077
The plan to cross Antarctica had failed

426
00:14:11,077 --> 00:14:11,637
completely

427
00:14:11,637 --> 00:14:14,037
But all twenty-eight men of the Endurance came

428
00:14:14,037 --> 00:14:15,157
home

429
00:14:15,881 --> 00:14:17,471
On the other side of the continent,

430
00:14:17,471 --> 00:14:19,951
the Ross Sea party had struggled to lay supply

431
00:14:19,951 --> 00:14:21,951
depots for a crossing that never came

432
00:14:21,951 --> 00:14:24,551
Three of its men did not return

433
00:14:25,818 --> 00:14:27,128
In 1922,

434
00:14:27,128 --> 00:14:29,328
Shackleton died of a heart attack on South

435
00:14:29,328 --> 00:14:29,848
Georgia,

436
00:14:29,848 --> 00:14:31,208
aged just forty-seven,

437
00:14:31,208 --> 00:14:33,608
and was buried at Grytviken

438
00:14:34,920 --> 00:14:35,910
As for the Endurance,

439
00:14:35,910 --> 00:14:37,830
for more than a century no one knew where

440
00:14:37,830 --> 00:14:40,230
she lay beneath the ice

441
00:14:40,836 --> 00:14:41,946
In 2022,

442
00:14:41,946 --> 00:14:44,906
the Endurance22 expedition took an icebreaker

443
00:14:44,906 --> 00:14:47,286
into the Weddell Sea and sent an underwater

444
00:14:47,286 --> 00:14:49,886
robot down to search the seabed

445
00:14:50,629 --> 00:14:51,579
On the fifth of March,

446
00:14:51,579 --> 00:14:52,619
2022,

447
00:14:52,619 --> 00:14:55,139
at a depth of three thousand and eight metres,

448
00:14:55,139 --> 00:14:57,739
the first images appeared on the screen

449
00:14:59,054 --> 00:15:00,604
Endurance was still upright,

450
00:15:00,604 --> 00:15:01,524
almost intact

451
00:15:01,524 --> 00:15:02,204
On her stern,

452
00:15:02,204 --> 00:15:04,164
the name ENDURANCE and the star were still

453
00:15:04,164 --> 00:15:05,284
clear

454
00:15:06,400 --> 00:15:08,750
The wreck lay only about seven kilometres from

455
00:15:08,750 --> 00:15:10,910
the position Captain Worsley had recorded in

456
00:15:10,910 --> 00:15:11,790
1915

457
00:15:11,790 --> 00:15:14,190
His calculations were astonishingly accurate

458
00:15:16,150 --> 00:15:17,060
No one touched the wreck

459
00:15:17,060 --> 00:15:19,300
It is protected as a historic monument of

460
00:15:19,300 --> 00:15:20,020
Antarctica,

461
00:15:20,020 --> 00:15:22,420
and will rest there forever

462
00:15:24,833 --> 00:15:26,483
Endurance is a story of failure

463
00:15:26,483 --> 00:15:28,503
But it is also a story of leaving no

464
00:15:28,503 --> 00:15:29,943
one behind

465
00:15:30,325 --> 00:15:32,035
If you were one of those twenty-eight men,

466
00:15:32,035 --> 00:15:33,875
would you sail on the James Caird with

467
00:15:33,875 --> 00:15:34,515
Shackleton,

468
00:15:34,515 --> 00:15:36,755
or stay and wait on Elephant Island?

469
00:15:36,755 --> 00:15:39,155
Tell me in the comments
